              earlier observation itself.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </details>
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-9">02.9 / Reveal the air through light</h3>
    <details>
      <summary>02.9 / Supplied horizon-light reference</summary>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-9-supplied-horizon-reference.jpeg"
          alt="Supplied ISS and Earth-horizon image, with a blue luminous atmospheric edge"
          width="2048"
          height="1363"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Supplied for this discussion, 16 November 2026. Visual inspiration for
          a luminous edge; original creator, provenance and depicted light
          mechanism are unverified. Not a study result.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </details>
    <p>
      The supplied ISS/horizon image suggests a luminous blue edge above visible
      cloud and ground. Its useful quality is light revealing an atmosphere
      without making its upper extent look like a material membrane. The image’s
      provenance and physical light mechanism are not established here; it is an
      appearance reference, not evidence that the depicted glow is ionization.
    </p>
    <p>
      The supplied atmosphere diagram is
      <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparison_US_standard_atmosphere_1962.svg"
        >Cmglee’s comparison of the 1962 standard atmosphere</a
      >. Its local .svg file is a saved Commons HTML page, rather than the
      vector itself. It distinguishes density and pressure from altitude; the
      study should not equate a bright outer limb with a dense upper shell.
      Looking tangentially through a layer gives a different optical path from
      looking straight through its face. Apparent edge brightness and material
      density are separate quantities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/why-nasa-watches-airglow-the-colors-of-the-upper-atmospheric-wind/"
        >NASA describes airglow</a
      >
      as light emitted by atmospheric gases excited by sunlight, with colour
      depending on gas and excitation. This supplies a second visual hypothesis
      alongside Sun-facing light. These examples are stylised cues, not optical
      simulations or a scientific identification of the supplied image. Their
      blue colour is provisional; no ionization or damage mechanic is implied.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Current line:</strong>
        the unchanged material reference from 02.7 and 02.8, preserving its
        useful crossing cue.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sunlit rim:</strong>
        a bright narrow core with a restrained glow, strongest toward the Sun
        and almost absent on the night side. Tests light revealing an edge
        without closing a bright circle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Airglow:</strong>
        the same core and glow with substantial night-side visibility. Tests an
        emitting upper layer that keeps the crossing visible away from the Sun,
        with a risk of reading as a shield.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All light proposals remove the upper material shell and retain only the
      existing low haze on World. The rim is cached fresh LFX using the shared
      hard-light pass, with no accumulated trail or animated pulse. It does not
      increase gas opacity, cloud coverage or physical density. The optical
      feather extends up to 3u beyond the unchanged 10u interaction depth; its
      bright core remains 0.7u inside that extent. This is a display cue, not an
      exact collision outline. The ship’s collision circle triggers entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hold Impact, 02.5 / Opaque clouds, Separate air, 1.45× rock shadow,
      rotation, depth, drag and burn fixed. Judge approach, skim and exit in
      Follow, then use Follow 4× to explain the core and glow. The question is
      whether emitted-looking light communicates air without a bubble, while
      retaining enough night-side visibility for play. Sunlit rim is the
      preferred reference, not a final placement or colour. Nearby engine and
      weapon illumination remains open.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>In-round finding · 16 November 2026:</strong>
      varying the rim’s brightness is the right direction. Sunlit rim is
      substantially better than the gas-volume options, but its brightest point
      directly toward the Sun feels misplaced. Explore two bright side regions,
      optionally shifted behind the tangents toward the night side, with subdued
      front and back. This is a visual hypothesis, not a claim about ionization
      physics.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Tangents:</strong>
        symmetric brightness peaks 90° from the Sun-facing axis, with an 18°
        falloff scale.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nightward:</strong>
        the same peaks moved 20° toward the night side, to 110° from the
        Sun-facing axis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Broad arcs:</strong>
        those same nightward centres, with a 36° falloff scale to test a more
        continuous sweep of light.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are two sides relative to the Sun, not planetary poles. The existing
      Sunlit rim and Airglow remain available. Blue colour, peak intensity,
      radial core/halo, low haze and opaque cloud layer are unchanged; the new
      options vary only angular brightness. Broad arcs has more total light
      because the peaks are wider. Colour and physical interpretation remain
      open rather than being inferred from an Earth-like identity.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Settled direction · 16 November 2026:</strong>
      retain the blue light profile and narrow paired arcs, with one final
      placement change: Dayward moves each tangent peak 20° toward the Sun, to
      70° from its direction. Nightward was the best built comparison, but
      Dayward is the selected direction for believability. Colour, radial core
      and halo, peak intensity and 18° angular falloff are settled for this
      example. This does not select a universal atmosphere colour for all
      planets. The remaining check is its response beside different star
      colours.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>02.9 / Retained placement references</summary>
      {["Sunlit rim", "Nightward"].map((name) => (
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-9-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-flight-reconstruction.png`}
            alt={`${name} at Follow scale`}
            width="1280"
            height="1000"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-9-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-close-reconstruction.png`}
            alt={`${name} at Follow 4×`}
            width="1280"
            height="1000"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Reconstructed 16 November 2026 before the Dayward colour check.
            Impact, 02.5 / Opaque, Separate air, shadow 1.45×, B1 0.50s; Follow
            and Follow 4× at 1280 × 1000. These preserve the built references;
            Dayward was selected as a modification, not seen here.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-10">02.10 / The blue Dayward rim beside different stars</h3>
    <p>
      Dayward fixes the selected narrow arcs 20° toward the Sun from each
      tangent. The slider changes the displayed star colour from 2300 to 12000
      K, opening at 5800 K. The star body and glow use interpolated anchors from
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/asna.202113868"
        >Harre and Heller’s stellar-colour study, Table 5</a
      >. This provides warm orange, near-white and blue-white references rather
      than a rainbow hue sweep. Interpolation and the game renderer are
      approximations, not a spectrum or a prediction of perceived astronomical
      brightness.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Fixed blue:</strong>
        the chosen rim’s light pixels remain unchanged. The test is whether that
        fixed emission looks coherent beside the changing star.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Star tint:</strong>
        an explicitly provisional response that filters the same blue emission
        by the star’s RGB channels. This may dim or shift the rim; it does not
        replace the selected source colour. It tests an alternative visual
        response, not atmospheric chemistry.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Compare 2300, 5800 and 12000 K at the same burn/time, first in Follow
      where the star and planet are visible together, then in Follow 4× for rim
      detail. The slider changes display colour only. Radius, pulse timing,
      orbit, gravity, charging, damage and drag remain fixed; temperature does
      not drive luminosity or ultraviolet output. Palette anchors use a
      normalised peak channel, so perceived brightness may vary with hue. The
      shared instrument uses the standard star body/glow for this override; the
      old G7 Sun HUD denotes the unchanged fixture, not spectral
      reclassification.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rock, clouds and low haze keep their existing light treatment. This
      isolates the rim response rather than simulating whole-scene illumination.
      Fixed emission staying blue is a model assumption, not evidence that real
      ionization is independent of a star’s spectrum. No response model is
      selected yet. The accepted cloud arrangement, Dayward geometry and blue
      source colour are held fixed throughout this check.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Decision · 16 November 2026:</strong>
      keep the fixed blue Dayward rim. Star tint is not needed; the chosen blue,
      narrow arcs and 20° dayward placement are settled for this reference.
      Further rim tuning is outside the next experiment. The star-colour check
      intentionally held rock and cloud lighting fixed, so it does not establish
      their response to coloured starlight. That material-light interaction
      remains a separate open item.
    </p>
    <InstrumentBand
      ><AtmosphereFlight
        label="Air around rock worlds: Dayward rim and star colour"
        client:load
      /></InstrumentBand
    >
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="03">03 / Readiness of the opaque-cloud arrangement</h2>
    <p>
      Scoped assessment · 16 November 2026. The independently useful result is a
      rotating rock with locally opaque clouds and clear openings, judged at
      flight scale. The study remains open in 02 for boundary design. A trial
      can retain the provisional pattern and colour; it need not wait for a
      texture library or variable star colours.
    </p>
    <p>
      Core already provides SphericalSurface and RockBodyPainter. The study
      composes those with a deterministic cloud atlas and Sun-facing cloud
      colour. Production’s flight-frame planet passes already support rotating
      Mercury and Venus materials, World bodies and Sun-facing LFX. However,
      this study’s opaque-alpha projector and separate rock-light composition
      live in study code, and its authored material loader is owned by the web
      Lab. Production has no generic adoption path for this combination yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      A trial needs an explicitly selected target body/mode and approved access
      to the reference rock materials, shared ownership of the cloud painter and
      composed material, and a production rendering/lifecycle check. Sustained
      cost for this CPU projection and multiple bodies has not been measured.
      Check pause, reset, camera scale and the interaction of the local
      rock-light composition with other LFX. The existing stock fallback must
      remain usable if material loading fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 10u air depth and 0.8/s drag are still an experimental Flight scene
      fixture. Production Venus’s atmosphere has no drag region. A visual trial
      must not silently adopt that mechanic or imply that its visible boundary
      already slows the ship. A playable braking encounter needs a separate
      explicit mechanics decision. This assessment authorises neither trial
      installation nor changes to production Venus.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="04">04 / A bounded game-trial scope</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No design-principle change is proposed.</strong>
      This scope preserves the successful material relationship within the
      current art direction. Sizes describe implementation breadth, not
      committed dates. Production implementation and deployment remain separate
      authorisations.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Define the trial — small.</strong>
        Select the target body and mode, choose 02.5 / Opaque or Sparse /
        Opaque, and state whether the trial is appearance-only. Record the
        provisional edge and colour in the planet spec. If configuration is
        needed, define its Zod contract before consumers. Depends on the target
        and trial decision.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Share the material composition — medium.</strong>
        Move the reusable cloud projection and rock/cloud composition into core
        assets, keeping authored inputs and experiment controls out of core.
        Provide production material loading and a fallback through the existing
        flight-frame setup. Match the retained Follow references and check
        opaque cores, clear openings and simulation-time rotation. Depends on
        the trial definition.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wire and assess the selected trial — medium.</strong>
        Mount through the existing World/LFX passes and canonical Practice
        planet preview where applicable. Check camera scales, pause/reset,
        existing ship controls and unchanged gravity/contact; measure sustained
        rendering cost at the intended body count. Revisit readiness if layering
        or performance requires a new renderer. Depends on shared composition.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Atmospheric drag/damage, new planet types, variable Sun colours,
      cloud-cast shadows, richer textures and a production texture library are
      outside these slices. Their unresolved status does not erase the usable
      cloud result; their adoption would require its own concrete decisions.
    </p>
  </section>
